A||||2||DJRice||Questions about Body work||||5
Z||000000||DJRice||10-05-1999||09:14 AM||||If I was going to try repainting a body panel that had no damage to it.  What do I need to do to prep it?  What grit sandpaper should I use to 'scuff' the paint and remove the pin stripes before priming and painting?  <P>Ive never tried any body work, and I just want to get an idea what Im getting in to when I give this a whirl.||||reg||5
Z||000001||RZURCTN||10-05-1999||01:26 PM||||Normally I would use a 400 grit sandpaper to prep the surface for repainting.  Wet-sanding works best.  After you sand and prime, use a 600 or higher paper to get the primer coat nice and smooth.  Be sure to wash with a mild soap solution and rinse well.  Use a tack rag to remove all dust and debris. Then paint in a well ventilated area.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-05-1999||03:46 PM||||You can also use some chemicals made specicifally for this.... the chemicals remove wax and other things. Napa and other paint suppliers should have something like this. I goes under a bunch of name.<P>use the chemical product before you sand, and again after you sand, then prime. That way the primer is going on a comletely wax/polish/tar/oil free surface. The chemicals will help clean any small areas you miss or can't get into with sanding.<P>If you leave any wax/polish at all behind the paint will start to peal at those spots. It's especially hard to clean well near trim w/o the chemical prep.||||reg||1
